WEBVTT

1
00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:08.352
That's a finished girth weld.

2
00:00:08.402 --> 00:00:12.522
In a week it'll be coated, lowered in and
buried under four feet of dirt.

3
00:00:12.733 --> 00:00:15.794
Twenty years from now, somebody's going to
ask three questions about it.

4
00:00:15.844 --> 00:00:16.630
Where is it?

5
00:00:16.680 --> 00:00:17.880
What went into it?

6
00:00:18.062 --> 00:00:19.242
Can you prove it?

7
00:00:19.292 --> 00:00:23.062
If you can answer all three for every weld
on the job, from the record and without

8
00:00:23.112 --> 00:00:26.036
digging anything up, you've got a weld
map.

9
00:00:26.177 --> 00:00:28.787
If you can't, you've got a pile of notes.

10
00:00:28.998 --> 00:00:33.094
Owners keep records like this for years,
often for the life of the line.

11
00:00:33.235 --> 00:00:37.562
Years from now, when a tool run inside the
pipe flags something, the first thing

12
00:00:37.612 --> 00:00:40.884
they'll ask is which weld that is and what
went into it.

13
00:00:41.095 --> 00:00:43.671
I've spent twenty years keeping these
records.

14
00:00:43.812 --> 00:00:47.130
This first lesson is about why they
matter.

15
00:00:48.233 --> 00:00:51.062
A weld map ties every weld to two things.

16
00:00:51.112 --> 00:00:53.386
Where it is, and what went into it.

17
00:00:53.783 --> 00:00:58.401
On most jobs it lives in a weld log like
this one, one line per weld.

18
00:00:58.612 --> 00:01:00.584
Here's one line from this spread.

19
00:01:00.725 --> 00:01:06.190
Weld number, FW-A-RT-0432.

20
00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:09.152
Station 486+40.

21
00:01:09.537 --> 00:01:14.818
Side A is pipe 2041, and Side B is pipe
2042.

22
00:01:14.947 --> 00:01:20.216
Their heats are Y60184 and Y60197.

23
00:01:20.345 --> 00:01:24.825
On pipe this size, welders brother-in-law
it, one on each side.

24
00:01:24.966 --> 00:01:29.457
W-031 and W-044 ran the root and the hot
pass.

25
00:01:29.668 --> 00:01:33.776
W-052 and W-058 ran the fill and the cap.

26
00:01:33.987 --> 00:01:40.800
The procedure is WPS-36-01, and the date
is 08-25-2026.

27
00:01:41.011 --> 00:01:44.736
When the film's been read, the NDE column
gets the result.

28
00:01:44.947 --> 00:01:48.439
Film 0432-A, accepted.

29
00:01:48.580 --> 00:01:51.654
And off the tally, each pipe's wall and
grade: 0.

30
00:01:51.704 --> 00:01:55.649
562 wall, X70, on both sides.

31
00:01:55.860 --> 00:01:57.517
Don't worry about every column yet.

32
00:01:57.567 --> 00:01:59.992
The next lesson goes through each one.

33
00:02:00.295 --> 00:02:02.591
For now, look at what one line does.

34
00:02:02.641 --> 00:02:06.491
It puts the weld in a place, and it ties
it to the steel, the welders, the

35
00:02:06.541 --> 00:02:08.920
procedure and the test.

36
00:02:10.033 --> 00:02:13.410
Here's what it looks like when the record
and the ground don't match.

37
00:02:13.806 --> 00:02:18.797
In 2010, a 30-inch gas line ruptured in
San Bruno, California.

38
00:02:19.008 --> 00:02:20.072
Eight people died.

39
00:02:20.122 --> 00:02:23.626
This is the NTSB's drawing of the pipe
that failed.

40
00:02:24.023 --> 00:02:29.964
In the middle are six pups, short pieces
of pipe welded end to end, from 3 ft 7 in

41
00:02:30.014 --> 00:02:32.345
to 4 ft 7 in long.

42
00:02:32.858 --> 00:02:36.047
The fracture started in the longitudinal
seam of pup 1.

43
00:02:36.097 --> 00:02:38.708
That's the weld that runs the length of a
pipe.

44
00:02:39.012 --> 00:02:41.702
On that pup, the seam was only partly
welded.

45
00:02:41.752 --> 00:02:46.787
The NTSB found that five of the six pups
wouldn't have met the quality standards of

46
00:02:46.837 --> 00:02:49.389
1956, when they went in.

47
00:02:49.786 --> 00:02:52.917
It found the seam defect would've been
visible at installation.

48
00:02:52.967 --> 00:02:57.340
A defect you could see got past quality
control, and inadequate quality assurance

49
00:02:57.390 --> 00:03:00.233
and quality control is part of the
probable cause.

50
00:03:00.537 --> 00:03:01.951
Now look at the records.

51
00:03:02.092 --> 00:03:07.359
PG&E's as-builts called this section
seamless X42 pipe with a 0.

52
00:03:07.409 --> 00:03:09.811
375-inch wall.

53
00:03:09.894 --> 00:03:11.866
That's not what was in the ground.

54
00:03:12.077 --> 00:03:17.785
After San Bruno, the NTSB asked for
records that are traceable, verifiable and

55
00:03:17.835 --> 00:03:18.958
complete.

56
00:03:19.008 --> 00:03:21.851
You'll hear those three words for the rest
of your career.

57
00:03:25.400 --> 00:03:27.859
So here's the standard I'll keep coming
back to.

58
00:03:28.001 --> 00:03:30.157
Call it the find-it-in-the-ground test.

59
00:03:30.207 --> 00:03:33.711
Take weld 0432 off the log.

60
00:03:33.852 --> 00:03:37.449
The log says it's at 486+40.

61
00:03:37.532 --> 00:03:40.920
Find that station on the alignment sheet,
then go stand on it.

62
00:03:41.224 --> 00:03:45.785
From the record, you should be able to
name the pipe on each side, both heats,

63
00:03:45.926 --> 00:03:49.721
the welders on each pass, the procedure
and the film.

64
00:03:49.862 --> 00:03:51.276
Now go the other way.

65
00:03:51.417 --> 00:03:55.908
You're standing on the right-of-way at
486+52.

66
00:03:56.049 --> 00:04:02.373
The map should tell you you're on pipe
2042, 12 feet past weld 0432, and

67
00:04:02.423 --> 00:04:04.256
everything that went into it.

68
00:04:04.467 --> 00:04:07.319
If both directions work, the map's doing
its job.

69
00:04:07.369 --> 00:04:09.340
If they don't, somebody digs.

70
00:04:09.390 --> 00:04:12.915
I've watched a crew excavate a quarter
mile of line to find one weld.

71
00:04:12.965 --> 00:04:18.211
And a weld you can't trace to its pipe,
its welders or its film can get cut out,

72
00:04:18.422 --> 00:04:20.172
because nobody can prove it's good.

73
00:04:20.222 --> 00:04:23.574
Every column on that log line is there so
this test passes.

74
00:04:23.624 --> 00:04:27.418
In the next lesson, we'll learn the words
you need to fill it in.
